one of the characteristic flaws of our  minds is to exaggerate how fragile we  might be to assume that life would be  impossible far earlier that is in fact  would be we imagined that we couldn't  live without a certain kind of income or  status or health that it would be a  disaster not to have a certain kind of  relationship house or job this natural  tendency of the mind is constantly  stoked by life in commercial society  which adds to our sense of the number of  things that must be considered  necessities rather than luxuries this  kind of society goes to extraordinary  lengths to get us to feel that we really  do need to go skiing once a year to have  heated car seats to fly in business  class to own the same kind of watch as a  famous conductor and have a jumbo sized  fridge and lay claim to lots of friends  perfectly muscular health and a loving  kind sex-filled relationship these  things would be nice but in fact our  core needs a much simpler than all this  we could in fact manage perfectly well  enough with a lot less not just around  possessions but across every aspect of  our lives it's not that we should want  to it's simply that we could we could  cope quite well with being rather poor  not very popular not having a very long  life and with living alone we could even  to put the extreme instance forward cope  with being dead it does happen  all the time but we forget our  resilience in a face of the risks we  face the cumulative effect of our  innocence is to make us  our lives become dominated by a fear of  losing with never getting things which  we could in fact do perfectly well  without the ancient Roman philosopher  Seneca had great success running what we  would now call a venture capital firm  he owned beautiful villas and  magnificent furniture but he made a  habit of regularly sleeping on the floor  of an outhouse and eating only stale  bread and drinking warm water he was  reminding himself that it wouldn't ever  be so bad to lose pretty much everything  so as to free himself from nagging  worries of catastrophe the realization  that he could be a penny gave him great  confidence he never worried so much for  that what might happen if a deal went  wrong because at the very worst he'd  only be back on the kitchen floor next  to the dog basket which was in the  scheme of things okay  Seneca was initiating an important move  by continually renewing our acquaintance  with our own resilience that is with our  ability to manage even if things go  badly or sacked a partner walking out a  scandal that destroys our social yards  an illness we can be braver because we  grasped that the dangers we face are  almost never as great as are skittish  imaginations tend to suggest in the  utopia our culture would stop  continuously presenting us with rags to  riches stories it would instead do  something far kinder and incidentally  far more conducive to the kind of  courageous entrepreneurial optimism that  our societies currently kinetically try  to foster our culture should be  continuously presenting us with charming  non tragic tales of riches-to-rags  stories stories in which people lost  money parks and social standing but  ended up coping really rather well with  their new lives we'd see them moving out  of the penthouse into a humble cottage  and having really rather a nice time  tending to a small flower bed and  discovering tinned food our culture  would not be recommending such scenarios  just lessening the grip upon us a  certain deep but misplaced fears that so  often hold us back  crying and succeeding we publish new  thought-provoking films every week be  sure to subscribe to our Channel and  take a look at more of what we have to  offer at the link on your screen now
 

